WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:01.727
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
We've all experienced

00:00:01.727 --> 00:00:03.390
the benefits of crowdsourcing.

00:00:03.390 --> 00:00:05.250
Whether it's
restaurant reviews that

00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:08.790
help us find a perfect place
for dinner or crowdfunding

00:00:08.790 --> 00:00:11.880
to bring our favorite
TV show back to life,

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:14.400
these distributed
contributions combined

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:16.920
to make some super useful tools.

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:19.530
What if we use that
same concept to build

00:00:19.530 --> 00:00:21.630
better machine learning models?

00:00:21.630 --> 00:00:23.566
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:23.566 --> 00:00:26.046
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:31.510 --> 00:00:35.390
Welcome to AI Adventures, where
we explore the art, science,

00:00:35.390 --> 00:00:36.850
and tools of machine learning.

00:00:36.850 --> 00:00:39.890
I'm Priyanka Vergadia,
and on this episode,

00:00:39.890 --> 00:00:42.910
we are going to examine a
different approach to machine

00:00:42.910 --> 00:00:43.750
learning.

00:00:43.750 --> 00:00:45.550
Standard machine
learning approaches

00:00:45.550 --> 00:00:47.830
require centralizing
the training data

00:00:47.830 --> 00:00:49.360
into a common store.

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:52.720
So let's say we want to train a
keyboard prediction model based

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:54.250
on user interactions.

00:00:54.250 --> 00:00:56.470
Traditionally, we
implemented intelligence

00:00:56.470 --> 00:00:59.980
by collecting all the data on
the server, create a model,

00:00:59.980 --> 00:01:01.420
and then serve it.

00:01:01.420 --> 00:01:04.780
Clients all talk to the
server to make prediction.

00:01:04.780 --> 00:01:08.950
The model and the data is
all in one central location,

00:01:08.950 --> 00:01:11.110
making it extremely easy.

00:01:11.110 --> 00:01:13.420
But the downside of
this centralized setup

00:01:13.420 --> 00:01:16.000
is that this back-and-forth
communication

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:18.760
can hurt the user
experience due to network

00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:21.850
latency, connectivity,
battery lives, and all sorts

00:01:21.850 --> 00:01:23.380
of unpredictable issues.

00:01:23.380 --> 00:01:27.310
One way to solve this is to have
each client independently train

00:01:27.310 --> 00:01:31.270
its own model using its own
data right on the device.

00:01:31.270 --> 00:01:33.430
No communication necessary.

00:01:33.430 --> 00:01:35.230
Great idea, right?

00:01:35.230 --> 00:01:38.740
Well, not quite because
each individual device

00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:41.770
does not have enough data
to render a good model.

00:01:41.770 --> 00:01:45.310
You could pre-train the model on
the server and then deploy it.

00:01:45.310 --> 00:01:49.150
But the problem with that is,
in our smart keyboard example,

00:01:49.150 --> 00:01:52.720
let's say if everyone started
using a new trendy word today,

00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:54.970
then the model trained
on yesterday's data

00:01:54.970 --> 00:01:56.510
won't be as useful.

00:01:56.510 --> 00:01:59.170
So what should we do
to utilize the goodness

00:01:59.170 --> 00:02:03.370
of decentralized data while
maintaining users' privacy?

00:02:03.370 --> 00:02:06.340
Well, that's where
federated learning comes in.

00:02:06.340 --> 00:02:08.919
The core idea behind
federated learning

00:02:08.919 --> 00:02:13.120
is decentralized learning,
where the user data is never

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:15.140
sent to a central server.

00:02:15.140 --> 00:02:17.290
So how does it work?

00:02:17.290 --> 00:02:20.110
Well, you start with
a model on the server,

00:02:20.110 --> 00:02:22.030
distribute it to the clients.

00:02:22.030 --> 00:02:25.000
But you can't just
deploy to every client

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:28.120
because you don't want to
break the user experience.

00:02:28.120 --> 00:02:30.460
You will identify
these clients based

00:02:30.460 --> 00:02:34.720
on which ones are available,
plugged in, and not in use.

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:37.270
Then, also find
out which ones are

00:02:37.270 --> 00:02:41.620
suitable because not all clients
will have the sufficient data.

00:02:41.620 --> 00:02:44.350
Once you've identified
suitable devices,

00:02:44.350 --> 00:02:46.990
we can then deploy
the model to them.

00:02:46.990 --> 00:02:50.710
Now, each client trains
the model locally using

00:02:50.710 --> 00:02:54.350
its own local data and
produces a new model,

00:02:54.350 --> 00:02:56.200
which is sent to the server.

00:02:56.200 --> 00:02:58.720
The thing to know here
is that the data used

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:01.540
to train the model on
the individual device

00:03:01.540 --> 00:03:03.250
never leaves the device.

00:03:03.250 --> 00:03:06.070
Only the weights, biases,
and other parameters

00:03:06.070 --> 00:03:08.920
learned by the model
leave the device.

00:03:08.920 --> 00:03:10.810
The server then
gets all the locally

00:03:10.810 --> 00:03:13.240
trained models and
averages them out,

00:03:13.240 --> 00:03:16.780
effectively creating
a new master model.

00:03:16.780 --> 00:03:19.240
But how do we know
that this process

00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:22.300
is doing something meaningful
and actually creating

00:03:22.300 --> 00:03:23.740
a good model?

00:03:23.740 --> 00:03:25.700
Doing this once is not enough.

00:03:25.700 --> 00:03:29.770
We have to do it over and over
so the combined model becomes

00:03:29.770 --> 00:03:32.110
the initial model
for the next round.

00:03:32.110 --> 00:03:34.780
And with every round,
the combined model

00:03:34.780 --> 00:03:37.360
gets a little bit better
thanks on the data

00:03:37.360 --> 00:03:39.070
from all those clients.

00:03:39.070 --> 00:03:42.130
And many, many rounds
later, your smart keyboard

00:03:42.130 --> 00:03:44.780
begins to show signs
of intelligence.

00:03:44.780 --> 00:03:48.220
Now, we just saw that all
the training data remains

00:03:48.220 --> 00:03:50.980
on your device and
no individual updates

00:03:50.980 --> 00:03:52.870
are stored in the cloud.

00:03:52.870 --> 00:03:55.640
For additional privacy
in federated learning,

00:03:55.640 --> 00:03:58.540
we can use the concept of
secure aggregation, where

00:03:58.540 --> 00:04:02.530
the server pairs up devices
with others in a buddy system.

00:04:02.530 --> 00:04:06.400
For example, here, each
device has two buddies.

00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:10.150
Data from each device can be
combined with the random values

00:04:10.150 --> 00:04:12.520
before being sent to the server.

00:04:12.520 --> 00:04:15.550
The server obviously knows
the value sent to the buddies

00:04:15.550 --> 00:04:18.970
and cancels them out to
just get the payload.

00:04:18.970 --> 00:04:23.500
This trick obfuscates the data
while in transit to the server.

00:04:23.500 --> 00:04:26.800
If you've used Google
Keyboard, the Gboard, then you

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:28.750
have already seen
and experienced

00:04:28.750 --> 00:04:30.610
a federated learning use case.

00:04:30.610 --> 00:04:32.950
The Gboard shows
a suggested query,

00:04:32.950 --> 00:04:35.590
your phone locally
stores information

00:04:35.590 --> 00:04:38.020
about the current
context and whether you

00:04:38.020 --> 00:04:39.490
clicked the suggestion.

00:04:39.490 --> 00:04:42.940
Federated learning processes
that history on device

00:04:42.940 --> 00:04:46.090
to suggest improvements to
the next iteration of Gboard's

00:04:46.090 --> 00:04:47.560
query suggestion model.

00:04:47.560 --> 00:04:50.230
In this episode, we learned
that federated learning

00:04:50.230 --> 00:04:52.630
is a collaborative and
decentralized approach

00:04:52.630 --> 00:04:55.900
to machine learning that allows
for smarter models, lower

00:04:55.900 --> 00:04:58.240
latency, and less
power consumption,

00:04:58.240 --> 00:05:00.940
all while ensuring user privacy.

00:05:00.940 --> 00:05:03.770
Federated learning is still
a relatively new concept,

00:05:03.770 --> 00:05:06.650
and there's definitely
more to come in the future.

00:05:06.650 --> 00:05:09.490
I would love to know some of
the federated learning use cases

00:05:09.490 --> 00:05:10.660
that you're thinking about.

00:05:10.660 --> 00:05:12.500
Drop them in the comments below.

